 "glint_note": "glint_deg is the angle between the line from an event to the satellite that saw it and the sunlight that a level surface at the event would reflect. At 0 the satellite sees the Sun's mirror image in still water or on a flat roof, so a flash seen near that geometry can be reflected sunlight rather than a bolide. The Sun's position comes from the low-accuracy method in chapter 25 of Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms (2nd edition, 1998), which he gives as accurate to 0.01 degree, and sidereal time from his chapter 12. The reflecting point is the event's catalogue latitude and longitude, on the WGS84 ellipsoid. glint_deg is null when the centre of the Sun was below the horizon, as it was for 36 of the 62 listed events.",
 "satellite_note": "The satellite's position comes from each event's catalogue record (attachments, satellite), because the record says where the satellite was when it saw that event. All 62 listed events were seen by one GLM each. The records place the satellite at latitude 0, 35786.02 km above the equator, at longitude 75.2 W. That is the GOES-East position, held first by GOES-16, which carries GLM-16, and then by GOES-19, which carries GLM-19. Where a record gives no position, the script takes GLM-16 and GLM-19 as GOES-East at 75.2 W; no listed event needed that.",
 "near_glint_note": "3 listed events lie within 2 degrees of glint: 685c240fc37fbbdc3dc012c6 (2025-06-23 16:44 UTC, 10.68 N, 73.12 W, 0.2 degrees), 68702784676cc8e13b244915 (2025-07-01 16:45 UTC, 10.67 N, 73.12 W, 0.1 degrees) and 68c59c0d02ba848d609b86e1 (2025-09-12 16:52 UTC, 1.53 N, 75.04 W, 1.0 degrees). Every other listed event seen in daylight is at least 19.9 degrees from glint. They stay in every count. near_glint_per_year counts, in each outline, the events whose glint_deg is 2 or less.",
 "events_note": "Every catalogued event inside the outline grown by 30 km. edge_km is the distance to the outline; inside says on which side of it the event lies. sat_lon is the longitude of the satellite that saw the event, and glint_note says what glint_deg is. The catalogue gives 30 of these positions to 0.1 degree only, about 11 km, the newest from 2024-02-23.",
